      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The original equation can be simplified by letting $y=\\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}$. We have:\n\n\\[y^2 + y = 12.\\]\n\nRearranging this equation gives us:\n\n\\[y^2 + y - 12 = 0.\\]\n\nFactoring the quadratic equation, we get:\n\n\\[(y + 4)(y - 3) = 0.\\]\n\nThus, the solutions are $y = -4$ and $y = 3$. \n\nNow, we need to consider the original equation:\n\n\\[\\left(\\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}\\right)=12.\\]\n\nLet $z=\\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}$. The equation now becomes:\n\n\\[z^2 + z = 12.\\]\n\nRewriting, we get:\n\n\\[z^2 + z - 12 = 0.\\]\n\nFactoring the quadratic equation, we get:\n\n\\[(z + 4)(z - 3) = 0.\\]\n\nThus, the solutions are $z = -4$ and $z = 3$. \n\nNow, we need to find the corresponding values of $x$ for each $z$. For $z = -4$:\n\n\\[-4 = \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}.\\]\n\nSolving for $x$, we get:\n\n\\[-4(3x - 4) = 4x - 16,\\]\n\\[-12x + 16 = 4x - 16,\\]\n\\[-12x - 4x = -16 - 16,\\]\n\\[-16x = -32,\\]\n\\[x = 2.\\]\n\nFor $z = 3$:\n\n\\[3 = \\frac{4x-16}{3x-4}.\\]\n\nSolving for $x$, we get:\n\n\\[3(3x - 4) = 4x - 16,\\]\n\\[9x - 12 = 4x - 16,\\]\n\\[9x - 4x = -16 + 12,\\]\n\\[5x = -4,\\]\n\\[x = -\\frac{4}{5}.\\]\n\nThus, the greatest possible value of $x$ is $\\boxed{-\\frac{4}{5}}$.",
